{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact E head 8f33ba6f6a1d646579d168cb6210251a132d99e6 on builder/typed-client-t0e-20261110, tree 6bd86d48, all 27 artifacts, for request d5fa31c9 under promise 230f5474 and review request 5d2d8b8b. Client gains `handle.intents.\u003ckind\u003e`, `formFor`, `slotsFor` and `actsOn`, and Define gains the `expression` tag. The head follows the three contracts of ruling 0be8be71.\n\nWhat I checked in the source:\n\n- Per-act methods. The `DeclaredHandle` constructor defines one enumerable own method per non-genesis act on a null-prototype object. `#fromSummary` clones the definition, the shaped input and the captured revisions, then reads the summary once. It returns `read`, `definition-mismatch` or `incomplete` before the signer runs, and it checks the digest even when the caller supplies `expected`. Captured revisions pass through unchanged. Otherwise `expectedOf` derives them, and each `via` subject in a final state comes from one bounded item query whose failure also returns before signing. The method checks no grant, state or guard, and `intent` and `submit` keep their bodies.\n- Moved helpers. `finalStates` and `effectiveFields` move from MCP `shape.ts` into Client `expected.ts` with the same bodies, and MCP re-exports them.\n- `formFor`, `slotsFor` and `actsOn`. `formFor` wraps the bound description functions from B and adds no wording or schema logic. MCP's `actTool` now takes its input schema from it. `slotsFor` returns the three declared groups only. `actsOn` lists transitions and comments on the item, sorted, and drops a kind only for an unconditional, top-level, primary-subject state guard. Nothing uses it to hide a tool or a method.\n- The tag. `expression(ctx)` accepts only a `{ field }` reference or a `{ slot, of }` reference that `BoundOf` now types with its subject. It finds the slot's one declared group, quotes names the profile needs quoted, and refuses a backtick. It returns a plain string for `rule`. The three wrong-name cases in `expression.types.ts` are compiler-only.\n- The witness. The Native Lending scenario now prepares every act through `handle.intents` against the real scope and submits with `handle.submit`. It keeps every refusal, final-item and publish assertion. The one new runtime case shows that a failed second read returns before the signer, with captured revisions given.\n- The seals. The head changes two existing hash literals, for Client `expected.ts` and MCP `catalogue.ts`. On my own extracted copy of the head, outside the builder's worktree, `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` and `scripts/active-source.test.mjs` pass all 24 cases.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main 1d7be4f3 is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 27 paths that its 27 artifacts cover. `git diff --check` passes, and the commit-message check passes: 8 checked, 0 flagged.\n- Byte equality. The strict comparison at 01337382 reports `docs/lanes-reference.md`, `docs/mcp-tools.json` and the three-mode Lending catalogue identical to their baselines. The first two digests equal the files committed at the head.\n- The gate. I read gate.exit at c370fcbb, tree aa3fccf5: \"exit 0\". The driver, launcher and analyzer exits read 0 too. The reconciliation records 5,839 passes, three expected skips, zero issues and zero unexpected cases. The failed first analyzer launcher and the failed metadata sealer stay recorded. Only the note and two evidence manifests change between c370fcbb and the head.\n- The browser. GitHub reports Page run 42846059399 attempt 1 as successful on push at 01337382, 12 of 12 steps. Between 01337382 and c370fcbb only the note and evidence manifests change. No changed path matches the Card workflow.\n- The evidence. My verifier reads six sealed file entries across the manifests. All match their sizes and digests and are read-only, and the credential scan finds no match. The earlier focused failures stay recorded beside the passing runs.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Client now owns preparation and the shared derivations, and MCP forwards to them instead of holding its own copies. Preparation stays separate from submission, so a kept packet remains the one retry path. The tag reuses the authoring references instead of a second naming scheme.\n\nSecurity: no finding. Preparation reads, shapes and signs, and it never submits. A failed or incomplete read stops before the signer, so nothing signs an empty revision map. The digest check runs on every call, and the scope still judges each grant, state and guard. `actsOn` rows grant nothing and hide nothing. The two seal changes cover moved code and one wrapper call, and every route and gate assertion stays the same.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Two notes:\n\n- `actsOn` in Client and `actsFor` in Lending's browser model (head D) filter acts by state guards in two slightly different ways. A later head could make Lending call `actsOn`.\n- `#fromSummary` clones the whole definition on every call. That is cheap at today's sizes and keeps a caller's later mutation out of the packet.\n\nThe Page asset changes. The planner decides the deployment and the live acceptance. No setting changes. This approval authorizes no deployment, publication or live acceptance.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:79ec5fe063d42ba34f6ca1d33e44a653e1811ecd","binding":"assigned","head":"8f33ba6f6a1d646579d168cb6210251a132d99e6","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2d836875339025a4aa35f70b58b7d4928911b408\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:230f5474cedda62059b5c9f4f89d11bb61a6f638\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2d836875339025a4aa35f70b58b7d4928911b408","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}